<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Supply Chain Innovation Observatory &middot; {{ quarter }}</title>
<style>
  :root{
    --ground:#F6F7F9; --surface:#FFFFFF; --sunk:#EDEFF3;
    --ink:#12171C; --body:#2B333B; --muted:#5C6874; --rule:#DCE1E6;
    --accent:#A85B12; --rise:#1E6E5E; --fall:#94402C;
    --display:ui-sans-serif,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
    --serif:Charter,"Iowan Old Style",Georgia,serif;
    --mono:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]){
      --ground:#0E1317; --surface:#161C22; --sunk:#121820;
      --ink:#E4E9ED; --body:#C3CCD5; --muted:#8A97A3; --rule:#262E36;
      --accent:#D08A3C; --rise:#4FA890; --fall:#C97155;
    }
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"]{
    --ground:#0E1317; --surface:#161C22; --sunk:#121820;
    --ink:#E4E9ED; --body:#C3CCD5; --muted:#8A97A3; --rule:#262E36;
    --accent:#D08A3C; --rise:#4FA890; --fall:#C97155;
  }
  *{box-sizing:border-box}
  body{margin:0;background:var(--ground);color:var(--body);font-family:var(--serif);
       font-size:16px;line-height:1.6;-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased}
  .wrap{max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 24px 80px}
  header{border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink);padding:48px 0 20px;margin-bottom:36px}
  .eyebrow{font-family:var(--display);font-size:11px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:.14em;
           text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent)}
  h1{font-family:var(--display);font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.03em;line-height:1.05;
     font-size:clamp(32px,6vw,52px);color:var(--ink);margin:12px 0 0}
  .facts{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:28px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);
         border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}
  .fact{flex:1 1 130px;padding:14px 18px 14px 0;border-right:1px solid var(--rule)}
  .fact:last-child{border-right:0}
  .fact b{display:block;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);
          font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
  .fact span{font-family:var(--display);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.08em;
             text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted)}
  section{margin:48px 0 0;max-width:64ch}
  h2{font-family:var(--display);font-weight:750;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--ink);
     font-size:24px;margin:0 0 6px}
  .source{font-family:var(--display);font-size:11px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:.12em;
          text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);margin:0 0 14px}
  p{margin:0 0 14px}
  .panel{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:3px;
         padding:20px 22px;margin:20px 0 0;overflow-x:auto}
  /* min-width so a narrow viewport scrolls the panel rather than clipping the
     last column against it. */
  table{width:100%;min-width:620px;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:var(--mono);
        font-size:13px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
  th{font-family:var(--display);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:.09em;
     text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);text-align:left;padding:0 12px 8px 0;
     border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);white-space:nowrap}
  td{padding:8px 12px 8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)}
  tr:last-child td{border-bottom:0}
  .name{font-family:var(--serif);font-size:15px;color:var(--ink);width:32%;min-width:200px}
  .num{text-align:right;width:1%;white-space:nowrap;color:var(--ink)}
  .dim{color:var(--muted)}
  .up{color:var(--rise);font-weight:600} .down{color:var(--fall);font-weight:600}
  .tag{font-family:var(--display);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.06em;
       text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);white-space:nowrap}
  /* A gated row is marked, not merely annotated. A number printed beside a
     caveat is still read as a number. */
  .tag.gated{color:var(--fall);font-weight:650}
  tr.gated .name{color:var(--body)}
  tr.gated .name::before{content:"\2022\00a0";color:var(--fall);font-weight:700}
  .withheld{font-family:var(--display);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.06em;
            text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);white-space:nowrap;
            text-align:right}
  .note{background:var(--sunk);border-left:3px solid var(--fall);padding:14px 18px;
        margin:18px 0 0;font-size:14.5px}
  .names{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin:14px 0 0;padding:0;list-style:none}
  .names li{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);border:1px solid var(--rule);
            background:var(--surface);padding:4px 8px;border-radius:2px}
  footer{margin:60px 0 0;padding:20px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);
         font-size:13.5px;color:var(--muted)}
  footer ul{margin:0;padding-left:18px} footer li{margin:0 0 6px}
  code{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.88em;background:var(--sunk);padding:1px 4px;
       border-radius:2px;color:var(--ink)}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">
<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Supply Chain Innovation Observatory &middot; {{ period_label }}</div>
  <h1>{{ quarter }}</h1>
  <div class="facts">
    <div class="fact"><b>{{ documents }}</b><span>documents matched</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><b>{{ rows|length }}</b><span>technologies seen</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><b>{{ silent|length }}</b><span>silent</span></div>
    <div class="fact"><b>{{ filers_total }}</b><span>filing companies</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

{% if partial %}
<section>
  <h2>This {{ period_noun }} is still filling up</h2>
  <p class="source">{{ weeks_run }} of {{ weeks_total }} weeks run</p>
  <p>The counts below cover {{ weeks_run }} of {{ weeks_total }} weeks, so they are
  not comparable with a full {{ period_noun }} and no share movement is reported. A shortfall
  of weeks looks exactly like a decline, which is why it is named here instead.</p>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section>
  <h2>What the {{ period_noun }} held</h2>
  <p class="source">{{ weeks[0] }} to {{ weeks[-1] }} &middot; lexicon version {{ lexicon_version }}</p>
  <p><strong>Index</strong> is 0&ndash;100: where a technology stands inside each
  kind of evidence, averaged and weighted by how much evidence each kind
  supplied. Counts are not comparable across sources &mdash; GitHub returned
  30,459 documents in 2026-Q3 against Hacker News's 2,304 &mdash; so the index
  says how a technology stands in what a source produced rather than how large
  the source is. It is withheld, like every other inference here, where one
  kind of evidence supplies almost all of it.</p>
  <p>Counts are documents matched, not mentions: a technology named twice in one
  filing counts once. The columns are kinds of evidence rather than databases:
  research covers preprints and journals together, and <em>other</em> gathers
  trade press, regulation, federal awards and community discussion. The
  concentration column names the kind that supplied most of the evidence, which
  is usually most of it.</p>
  {% if single_source_count %}
  <p class="note"><strong>{{ single_source_count }} of {{ rows|length }}
  technologies</strong> &mdash; holding {{ single_source_documents }} of
  {{ documents }} documents &mdash; rest on a single kind of evidence. They are
  marked below, and this {{ period_noun }}'s movement is withheld for them. A
  share computed from one source measures that source's coverage, not the
  technology; the counts stand, the movement does not.</p>
  <p>Diversity is counted across <em>kinds</em> of evidence rather than source
  names, because two sources measuring the same thing are one piece of evidence
  twice: research (preprints and journals), code, patents, filings, trade press,
  regulation, federal awards and community discussion. A technology is marked
  when one kind supplies {{ (single_source_share * 100)|round|int }}% or more of
  its documents, or when fewer than two kinds reach
  {{ family_floor }} document{{ "s" if family_floor != 1 }}.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<div class="panel">
  <table>
    <thead><tr>
      <th class="name">Technology</th><th class="num">Index</th><th class="num">Docs</th>
      <th class="num">Research</th><th class="num">Code</th>
      <th class="num">Patents</th><th class="num">Filings</th>
      <th class="num">Other</th><th class="num">Filers</th>
      <th class="tag">Concentration</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in rows %}
      <tr{% if row.single_source %} class="gated"{% endif %}>
        <td class="name">{{ row.name }}</td>
        <td class="num{% if row.index is none %} dim{% endif %}">{% if row.index is none %}&mdash;{% else %}{{ row.index|round|int }}{% endif %}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ row.total }}</td>
        {# Families, not sources. Eight source columns will not fit, and the
           gate already reasons in families -- arXiv and Scopus are one kind of
           evidence however many databases supply it. #}
        {% set other = (row.by_family.get('trade', 0) + row.by_family.get('regulation', 0)
                        + row.by_family.get('money', 0) + row.by_family.get('community', 0)) %}
        <td class="num{% if not row.by_family.get('research') %} dim{% endif %}">{{ row.by_family.get('research', 0) }}</td>
        <td class="num{% if not row.by_family.get('code') %} dim{% endif %}">{{ row.by_family.get('code', 0) }}</td>
        <td class="num{% if not row.by_family.get('patents') %} dim{% endif %}">{{ row.by_family.get('patents', 0) }}</td>
        <td class="num{% if not row.by_family.get('filings') %} dim{% endif %}">{{ row.by_family.get('filings', 0) }}</td>
        <td class="num{% if not other %} dim{% endif %}">{{ other }}</td>
        <td class="num{% if not row.filers %} dim{% endif %}">{{ row.filers }}</td>
        <td class="tag{% if row.single_source %} gated{% endif %}">{{ row.concentration }}% {{ row.top_family }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

{% if substance_rows %}
<section>
  <h2>Substance and attention</h2>
  <p class="source">Documents that are building against documents that are talking</p>
  <p>Horizontal is attention &mdash; trade press and community discussion.
  Vertical is substance &mdash; code, patents, filings, federal awards and
  regulation. Research sits in neither: a preprint is not a built thing and it
  is not hype, and nine hundred of them would drown the distinction. A
  technology above the diagonal has more being built than said about it.</p>
</section>
<div class="panel">
  {{ substance_chart }}
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if map_points %}
<section>
  <h2>Build Map</h2>
  <p class="source">{{ map_points|length }} located federal awards this {{ period_noun }}</p>
  <p>Every award with a place of performance, sized by the square root of its
  obligated dollars so a hundred-million-dollar award is ten times a
  million-dollar one rather than a hundred. An award whose dollars were not
  reported still gets a dot &mdash; it is a place where capacity is being built.
  The frame is the continental United States; Alaska and Hawaii are drawn
  clamped to the edge rather than dropped.</p>
</section>
<div class="panel">
  {{ build_map }}
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if risers %}
<section>
  <h2>What moved against {{ previous }}</h2>
  <p class="source">Share of the {{ period_noun }}'s documents, in percentage points</p>
  <p>Share rather than count, because the corpus itself is not stable from one
  {{ period_noun }} to the next. A technology can gain documents and still lose ground.</p>
</section>

<div class="panel">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th class="name">Technology</th><th class="num">Docs</th><th class="num">Shift</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in risers %}
      <tr><td class="name">{{ row.name }}</td><td class="num">{{ row.total }}</td>
          <td class="num up">+{{ '%.1f'|format(row.shift) }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
      {% for row in fallers %}
      {% if row.shift < 0 %}
      <tr><td class="name">{{ row.name }}</td><td class="num">{{ row.total }}</td>
          <td class="num down">{{ '%.1f'|format(row.shift) }}</td></tr>
      {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{% elif not partial %}
<section>
  <h2>Nothing to compare against</h2>
  <p>{{ previous }} holds no observations, so no share can have moved. A technology
  cannot have risen against a {{ period_noun }} that was never observed.</p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if silent %}
<section>
  <h2>Silent this {{ period_noun }}</h2>
  <p class="source">Zero documents across all six sources</p>
  <p>A silent entry means either an absent technology or a pattern that cannot see
  it. The distinction is settled by probing the corpus directly, not by waiting.</p>
</section>
<ul class="names">
  {% for tech in silent %}<li>{{ tech.name }}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endif %}

<footer>
  <ul>
    <li>Across this corpus two thirds of technology-weeks hold zero observations, so a
    weekly ranking mostly reports which week a collector caught something. Collection
    stays weekly — it is the only window in which every query stays under its API's
    result cap — and only the reporting is aggregated.</li>
    <li>No momentum. It was the only metric needing a time series, and the one that
    reported noise as trend: 95% of the signals table is observed zeros, so a
    technology seen once still scored. What remains is cross-sectional.</li>
{% if licensed %}
    <li><strong>Licensed sources.</strong> {{ licensed|join(', ') }} reached this report
    through a hand-made export from a database this institution subscribes to. Counts and
    citations are published; the licensed text is not, and is never stored. Following a
    citation from those sources needs a subscription of your own.</li>
{% endif %}
    <li>Matching is deterministic. The pattern that fired is stored on every
    observation, so any number here can be traced to its evidence.</li>
  </ul>
</footer>
</div>
</body>
</html>
